answers.answer:
Relevant legislation is the applicable law of the country where the commodity was produced—grown, harvested, obtained, or raised on relevant plots of land (or, for cattle, in establishments) [EC Guidance §6(a) (part 1)][EC FAQ Q3.3 (part 1)]. It covers laws concerning the legal status of that production area, including:

- land-use rights;
- environmental protection;
- forest management and biodiversity conservation directly related to wood harvesting;
- third parties’ rights;
- labour rights;
- internationally protected human rights;
- free, prior and informed consent (FPIC); and
- tax, anti-corruption, trade and customs rules [EUDR Art. 2(40)].

The list may include national laws, relevant secondary law, and international law applicable domestically, but only insofar as they concern the area’s legal status [EC Guidance §6(a) (part 1)][EC FAQ Q3.3 (part 1)]. Laws of countries where later manufacturing occurs do not count for this legality requirement [EC FAQ Q3.3 (part 1)][EC FAQ Q1.29.1].

